{
  "image_name": "5990d9803e1280e8b7451cb4261f0df2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how nitrogen is transformed and exported to the sea in three coastal settings: sandy, muddy, and rocky coasts. Across all cases, groundwater discharge links land-based nitrogen sources with coastal waters, but the dominant pathways differ with sediment structure and permeability.\n\nIn sandy coasts, permeable sediments allow strong exchange between groundwater and seawater, so infiltrating organic matter is mineralized and then nitrified. The diagram suggests brackish submarine groundwater discharge often carries nitrogen mainly as nitrate, with some ammonium and dissolved organic nitrogen also cycling through denitrification and related reactions.\n\nIn muddy coasts, low permeability is offset by burrows that create secondary pathways and redox mosaics. Here, plant inputs, soil organic matter, anaerobic mineralization, denitrification, DNRA, and nitrification all interact. The exported groundwater appears more mixed, with saline discharge dominating over fresh discharge and carrying substantial dissolved organic nitrogen and ammonium.\n\nIn rocky coasts, groundwater moves through fractures, conduits, and submarine springs rather than reactive sediments. Because contact with biologically active material seems limited, nitrate attenuation is weak and only minor ammonium or dissolved organic nitrogen production occurs. Fresh groundwater discharge dominates and the exported nitrogen is shown chiefly as nitrate.",
